Z||000006||Haze_Performance||11-03-2000||05:52 AM||||I have Poly in my V6 (Auto) Its fine.<P>But my I4 (Auto) was brutal. I put a new stock one in and all the vibes stoped. A little more info: My car had all new motor/trans mounts and only 33,000 miles on it.<P>If you buy one I wish you good luck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlogo1_1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000007||HAGS||11-03-2000||06:11 AM||||I replaced the dog bone with an OEM Rubber Dog bone and also replaced the engine mount the day after I bought the car...it lasted good for about a month but then I realized my air filter was getting damaged from hitting the back of the car...seems my only option is going Poly or cutting out some of the trunk to allow the engine to rock back more.  I have front transmission mount if anyone needs it (Part #52910 in Fierostore cat)(very cheap, basically shipping).||||reg||1
Z||000008||MrPBody||11-03-2000||07:54 AM||||As I have an '85 4-cylinder, I'm not familiar with the '86 mount system -- but that won't stop me from talking about it.<P>I installed an aftermarket dogbone from Autozone.  Quality is OK but not great, and the stock-design end slots in its bushings allow too much movement for my taste.  To stiffen it, I stuffed its slots with slices of heater hose to make effectively solid rubber bushings.  This sounds crude, but it works pretty well.  The engine has much less rocking slop, and I feel only a little more vibration -- slight mirror buzz under some conditions.  Low-tech can be good!<P>Good luck with your headbanger.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
